<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Weapons</h1>
<p>Every weapon is of a type, and each type is a skill of its own: long 
sword, dagger, battle axe, spear, and so on. Look at a weapon to see its 
type ("The long sword clearly requires a long sword fighting style"); in 
shops it is shown in brackets in the list.
</p><p>Your skill in a type decides two things:
</p>
<ul><li><b>How well you attack with it.</b> A weapon you haven't practiced is clumsy in your hands.</li>
<li><b>How well you defend against it.</b> Half your skill in the weapon your opponent swings helps you parry it, so knowing a weapon also guards you against it.</li></ul>
<p>Practice weapon skills at your guild (see <a cmd='#'>Help Practice</a>); each guild 
teaches its own weapons (see Help Fighter Weapons, Help Thief Weapons and 
the like). <a cmd='#'>status weapons</a> shows what you know.
</p><p>The types fall into six families:
</p>
<ul><li><b>Axes and hammers</b>: battle axe, hand axe, war mattock, adze mattock, maul, war hammer, brass knuckles.</li>
<li><b>Swords</b>: great sword, claymore, long sword, broad sword, falchion, scimitar, short sword, rapier, main gauche, stiletto, dagger, sickle.</li>
<li><b>Clubs and maces</b>: club, battle club, mace, battle mace, morning star, flail.</li>
<li><b>Polearms</b>: quarterstaff, spear, javelin, lance, trident, halberd, bardiche, scythe.</li>
<li><b>Natural</b>: fist, kick, elbow and knee, and the claws, bites and stings of beasts.</li>
<li><b>Special</b>: whip, and the bow, crossbow and sling, which shoot (see <a cmd='#'>Help Shoot</a>).</li></ul>
<p>Most weapons lean on strength, with some dexterity; the whip, the sickle 
and the bows and slings lean on dexterity (see <a cmd='#'>Help Strength</a> and 
<a cmd='#'>Help Dexterity</a>). Two-handed weapons are slower and leave no hand free 
for a shield; light ones strike faster. Each weapon also has a help page 
of its own: help and the weapon's name.
</p><p><br />
